ef3ecf2d-b636-40d3-b622-42d6b5034ec2,,"From Production to Physics: Agents of today, Quantum of tomorrow!",Topic 1,"Topic 1
Title : Trusted by Design: memory, autonomy and the agent
Abstract : AI Agents are moving from demos to production — and the obvious question: how much should an agent remember about you, your system, and the past outcomes and decisions? And how much freedom should it have to act on that knowledge? lets explore the two hardest unsolved problems in agentic AI: memory (What agents should remember and what goes wrong when memory is poorly designed) and autonomy (how to choose the right level of autonomy based on stakes, reversibility and trust) , and why those are deeply connected.

Topic 2
Title : Quantum-proof your Java: A practical starting point
Abstract : Every login, signature and encrypted message you ship today relies on MATH that a large quantum computer could break easily. Attackers are already stealing encrypted data to crack it later: called ""Harvest now, Decrypt later"". This means the clock is already running.
What we’ll address:
Quantum computing in plain English
Why RSA and ECC will not survive quantum
What Java gives you today for post-quantum crypto
How to start your migration
Let’s learn where we are exposed and what to do first.",,2026-10-28 11:00:00+00,,Asia/Kolkata,online,Online event,,,c78f921f-ce89-4a92-b98f-83bf7cc57102,"{technology,science,ai}",,external,https://www.meetup.com/polyglot-languages-runtimes-java-jvm-nodejs-swift/events/316957087/,Register on Meetup,f,,t,,,,GBP,published,,f,f,2026-10-22 03:22:46.934486+00,source:a123de80-56fb-42df-942f-e46132c725f9,https://www.meetup.com/polyglot-languages-runtimes-java-jvm-nodejs-swift/events/316957087/,event_316957087@meetup.com,,2026-10-22 03:22:12.257+00,,2026-10-22 03:22:46.934486+00,2026-10-22 03:22:54.857806+00,,,,,f,
df4a8ee7-eba2-4d2e-9c8b-b6cff8ab0257,,7th Frankfurt Meetup – Prompts Are Not Controls: Governing Agentic QA,What happens when an AI test agent ignores its instructions but still reports a successful test run?,"What happens when an AI test agent ignores its instructions but still reports a successful test run?

As AI agents take on more testing responsibilities, from generating tests and executing suites to triaging failures and reporting results, relying on prompts alone is no longer enough. Quality requires enforceable boundaries, verifiable evidence, and clear accountability.

At The Test Tribe’s 7th Frankfurt Meetup, Muthu Kumar Koodalingam will demonstrate how to make AI-powered testing agents more secure, auditable, and reliable through engineering controls rather than instructions alone.

***Session***
***Prompts Are Not Controls: Governing Agentic QA***

***Speaker: Muthu Kumar Koodalingam (https://www.linkedin.com/in/muthukumark12), QA Automation Engineer at GLS IT Services***

With over 14 years of experience across mobile, web, and API testing, Muthu specializes in test automation strategy, developer tooling for QA, and applying engineering discipline to AI-assisted testing. His current work focuses on making AI agents in test automation enforceable, auditable, and verifiable.

This session explores why system prompts, AGENTS.md files, and skill instructions cannot serve as security controls on their own. Through a practical demonstration, Muthu will show how to define agent permissions, enforce restrictions at runtime, independently verify test results, and introduce human approval gates to keep agentic QA accountable.

**The session will cover:**

• Enforcing Agent Boundaries: Defining agent authority through configuration, restricting write access by path, and removing unnecessary tools rather than relying on instructions.
• Runtime Controls and Verification: Checking every tool call, reading back effective permissions, and verifying that agents operate within their authorised boundaries.
• Trustworthy Test Results: Deriving pass/fail outcomes from exit codes and captured output instead of trusting an agent’s summary.
• Independent Review and Human Oversight: Using independent model reviews, deterministic checks, and human approval gates to catch failures before they move to the next stage.

The central question to take back to your team: If your test agent ignored its instructions, what would stop it?

***Event Details***

• ***Date: 11 November 2026***
• ***Time: 05:30 PM – 07:30 PM Local Time***
• ***Venue: Ederstraße 10, 60486 Frankfurt am Main-Innenstadt II, Germany***

**Agenda**

• 5:30 PM – 5:45 PM: Welcome & Introductions
• 5:45 PM – 6:45 PM: Speaker Session by Muthu Kumar Koodalingam on “Prompts Are Not Controls: Governing Agentic QA”, including Q&A and demo
• 6:45 PM – 7:30 PM: Networking over snacks
• 7:30 PM: Event Wrap

*Stay back after the session to exchange ideas with fellow QA and Engineering professionals on building agentic testing systems that can be trusted through evidence, enforcement, and accountability.*

**About The Test Tribe**

The Test Tribe is the world's largest software testing community, empowering testers globally since 2018. With 925+ events and 170K+ members across 130+ countries, we bring software professionals together to learn, grow, and connect through meetups, conferences, cohorts, masterclasses, and more.

**Become a Speaker at The Test Tribe Events**

Wish to share your insights with the community at a future event?

Submit your talk ideahere (https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSc8xebmOBD2WYXwGKWj7GteY-tMLaj-aZzQk88MAsE0LUiMUQ/viewform?utm_source=luma), and if it resonates with the perspective we are trying to build at our events, our team will connect with you.

By RSVPing, you agree to ourTerms & Conditions and Privacy Policy (https://www.thetesttribe.com/privacy-policy/?utm_source=luma) and consent to be contacted by The Test Tribe and our event partners.
