a30295cf-7aec-48d6-b62d-25124ed67b53,,Demystifying Weather Apps: From Synoptic Charts to Digital Screens,An Online Workshop for Windsports Lovers,"**An Online Workshop for Windsports Lovers**
Registration for this event can be found on our website (https://allwindsadventures.com/events/demystifying-weather-apps-from-synoptic-charts-to-digital-screens):
The link to the registration will be sent once your position is confirmed.

If you want to know exactly how to use weather apps for sailing effectively, this workshop is for you.
Ever heard someone at the marina or the beach exclaim, *“The app was completely wrong today!”*? Weather apps aren’t broken—they are powerful computational engines that are frequently misunderstood. In this interactive online session, we demystify how digital weather models process atmospheric data, why forecast screens seem to fail near coastlines and mountains, and how to combine traditional seamanship with screen data to make confident, safe calls on the water.
Whether you sail on Lake Zug, windsurf, or plan offshore charters, this workshop will give you a clear framework for reading weather data without getting overwhelmed by screens.

**What We Will Cover**
**1. How We Used to Forecast (And Why It Still Matters)**

• The fundamentals of synoptic meteorology: high/low-pressure systems, cold/warm fronts, and isobar gradients.
• Reading synoptic weather charts before screens existed—and why tracking barometric pressure trends onboard remains your ultimate real-time early warning system.

**2. Inside the Digital Weather App**

• What apps like Windy *actually* display vs. what people think they display.
• Understanding the difference between sustained ground wind (a 10-minute averaged mathematical vector 10m above water) and peak gusts (3-second localised spikes).
• **The 3 Workflows:** How my weather routing changes completely depending on where we are sailing:
• **Lake Zug:** Why global apps struggle with our local topography and how to read the Bise and Föhn.
• **The Mediterranean:** Tracking convective squalls, sea breezes, and reading the warning signs of a Bora or Jugo.
• **The Atlantic:** How we rely on synoptic fronts and larger weather systems.

**3. Global vs. Regional Models (The Resolution Problem)**

• **Global Models (ECMWF, GFS):** Broad grid squares that smooth out islands, headlands, and mountain passes.
• **Regional High-Res Models (ICON-EU, AROME):** Tight grids that capture coastal terrain compression and localised convective activity.
• How model divergence (when ECMWF and GFS disagree) highlights atmospheric instability.

**4. Why “The App is Always Wrong” (And How to Fix Your Setup)**

• **Terrain Acceleration:** How steep hills and narrows double the wind speed shown on a global grid model.
• **The CAPE Factor:** Why flat, quiet forecast screens can hide explosive thunderstorm potential. For a deep dive on coastal squalls, read our guide on Surviving the Dalmatian Nevera [link].
• Setting up custom screens in tools like Windy or MeteoSwiss to cross-reference multiple models simultaneously. Check out our breakdown on Weather Apps for Sailing [link] for background reading before the event.

**Session Details**

• **Date & Time:** November 10, 2026 | 19:30 – 21:00 CEST
• **Format:** Live Interactive Online Workshop (60 mins presentation + 30 mins open Q&A)
• **Cost:** FREE with account registration (or CHF 15 for non-registered guests)

**Language:** The event is run in English. However, if you are a native German speaker and worried about missing technical nuances, don’t be. I speak German and can easily translate basic concepts if necessary.

*Insurance is the responsibility of the participant; by RSVPing “yes”, you agree to act responsibly at all times at this event and to not hold Swiss Alpine Adventure/All Winds Adventures or any person involved responsible for any injury or loss which may occur in relation to the event.*

**Disclaimer:**
All participants take part in events or activities facilitated by Swiss Alpine Adventure/All Winds Adventures entirely at their own risk. Swiss Alpine Adventure/All Winds Adventures does not accept liability for damage to property, death or personal injury that may occur whilst taking part in activities or events organised by Swiss Alpine Adventure/All Winds Adventures. It is your responsibility to make sure your equipment is maintained and in safe working order. We reserve the right to stop you from participating with us if your equipment is badly maintained or not in safe working order. Whilst we endeavour to make sure that the information on this website pertaining to routes and trails is correct, we do not warrant its completeness or accuracy.",https://secure.meetupstatic.com/photos/event/4/2/c/5/highres_535877093.jpeg,2026-11-10 18:30:00+00,,Europe/Zurich,online,Online event,,,8827b689-2637-4245-8ba7-214b35948ea2,"{sport,fitness,travel}",,external,https://www.meetup.com/allwindsadventures/events/316315843/,Register on Meetup,f,,t,,,,GBP,published,,f,f,2026-10-22 19:54:29.549376+00,source:f26eb5d4-b239-4813-8e0c-3cf689eed4bd,https://www.meetup.com/allwindsadventures/events/316315843/,event_316315843@meetup.com,,2026-10-28 03:22:30.379+00,,2026-10-22 19:54:29.549376+00,2026-10-28 03:22:55.486349+00,,,,,f,
40fc0bec-2bf2-4461-8394-8f25678702a2,,Nordic Walking Brockwell Park,Ready to get outside? Our Friday morning Nordic Walk in Brockwell Park is back!,"Ready to get outside? Our Friday morning Nordic Walk in Brockwell Park is back!

At this moment we can meet with up to 30 people (of more than two households) to do outdoor exercise and walks. However, i like to keep our exercise groups small, so I have set the maximum participants at 12.

For now this walk is for our experienced Nordic Walkers only, who already used to Nordic Walk with us regularly before Covid 19 kept us indoors, and who have their own Nordic Walking poles.

As this session is outdoors, we will not ask you to wear a face mask, but we do ask you to keep 2 meters apart. Some of our walkers might be immune compromised or more at risk, so please help us keep everyone safe!

Please do not come to the session and stay at home if:
• you know you have been in contact - in the last 2 weeks- with someone with symptoms of possible Covid, or if you have been in contact with someone who tested positive.
• you have any of the following symptoms: (new) Loss of smell/taste, Persistent cough, Fatigue, Loss of appetite, Skin rash, Hives, Fever, Severe muscle pain, Shortness of breath, Diarrhoea, Delirium, Abdominal pain, Chest pain, Hoarse voice, Eye soreness, Sore or painful throat, Nausea or vomiting, Headache, Dizziness or light headedness

We meet by the Blackbird bakery in Herne Hill, between the station and the park.

Session Price: £10 per session, £89 for a 10-session card or £70 unlimited sessions per month. Session cards and Pay Monthly can be used in any of our outdoor walks or indoor Zoom classes sessions (except for the Parkinson's sessions).

Pay What Works:
The price shown above is the “true” value of the session that best reflects the work I put into preparing and leading your session. However, I understand that not everyone can afford to pay full price, and I don’t want that to stop you from joining us.

In an effort to find a balance between more financial accessibility and sustainable pricing, I now offer a “Pay What Works” model. This allows for financial flexibility while still valuing and respecting the work and energy I provide in creating and delivering these classes.

Then send an email to ailien@fitandhappyoutdoors.com with the heading “INVOICE please”- followed by “FAHO 1” to pay full price as quoted below, “FAHO 2”, for 20% off, or “FAHO 3” for 50% off.

Please do tell me if you want to pay for 10 sessions, or if you’d like to pay monthly.
I prefer payment by bank transfer, but if that is not possible you could pay in cash. No cheques please, the queues by the banks are just too long.

