5f304f58-64cd-4c90-bb39-e17ba687ebc0,,Build a Reasoning Model (From Scratch),We are working through Build a Reasoning Model (From Scratch) by Sebastian Raschka.,"We are working through *Build a Reasoning Model (From Scratch)* by Sebastian Raschka.
During each meetup, we will discuss the key concepts from the chapter or pages under review. In this session we'll discuss the **Chapter 5. Inference-time scaling**
**via self-refinement.**

Raschka provides a hands-on guide to understanding and building modern reasoning models. Starting with a conventional pretrained LLM, the book explores evaluation, inference-time reasoning techniques, reinforcement learning with verifiable rewards, and knowledge distillation.

Please note that the session is not recorded, and participants are responsible for obtaining their own copy of the book.

**Buy the book** (affiliate links)**:**
Amazon UK (https://amzn.to/4qecrce)
Amazon France (https://link.amazon/B00LK2CWI)

**Book overview:**
In *Build a Reasoning Model (From Scratch)*, bestselling author Sebastian Raschka explains how modern reasoning-oriented language models work by implementing their core techniques step by step.
You will begin with a conventional pretrained LLM and learn how to generate and evaluate its responses. You will then explore inference-time techniques such as chain-of-thought prompting, sampling, self-consistency, response scoring, Best-of-N, and self-refinement. Later chapters introduce training-based approaches, including reinforcement learning with verifiable rewards, GRPO, format rewards, and distillation from stronger reasoning models into smaller ones.
*Build a Reasoning Model (From Scratch)* teaches you how to:

• Implement core LLM reasoning techniques from scratch
• Generate, score, and evaluate model responses
• Build verifier-based evaluation systems
• Improve reasoning using self-consistency, Best-of-N, and self-refinement
• Apply reinforcement learning with verifiable rewards
• Understand and implement GRPO-based training
• Distill the reasoning capabilities of stronger models into smaller ones
• Evaluate the accuracy, cost, and latency trade-offs of different reasoning techniques",https://secure.meetupstatic.com/photos/event/8/8/6/b/highres_535654923.jpeg,2026-10-25 15:30:00+00,,Europe/London,online,Online event,,,4cece675-2670-4581-90e6-2b792576b047,{technology},,external,https://www.meetup.com/llm-reading-club/events/316895591/,Register on Meetup,f,,t,,,,GBP,published,,f,f,2026-10-21 22:16:55.807095+00,source:a123de80-56fb-42df-942f-e46132c725f9,https://www.meetup.com/llm-reading-club/events/316895591/,event_316895591@meetup.com,,2026-10-25 03:23:03.148+00,,2026-10-21 22:16:55.807095+00,2026-10-25 03:23:38.087622+00,,,,,f,
1bfd8eb6-e238-498a-b964-06385de3eaf1,,Jean-Paul Sartre on Freedom and the Other.,TORONTO PHILOSOPHY MEETUP - Online.,"**TORONTO PHILOSOPHY MEETUP** - Online.
Hosted by Alex.
Please RSVP on their page:
https://www.meetup.com/the-toronto-philosophy-meetup/events/316267189/

We will be reading **Sartre's** account of the concrete ways a free subjectivity relates toward the Other. The readings are from Part 3, Chapter 3 of *Being and Nothingness* as follows.

**October 24:** ""Being-with (*Mitsein*) and the 'we'.""
(pp. 591-613 in PDF)
⸻ ♦ ♦ ♦ ⸻
So-called ‘enlightened societies’ seem to enter periods of crisis, war and unspeakable violence on a regular basis. Can these events simply be attributed to “the crooked timber of humanity, out of which no straight thing was ever made"" (Kant), or is there a deeper, more sinister reason for their occurrence? Could it be that the Enlightenment itself – its rationality, moral outlook and existential condition – possesses a disavowed “dark underbelly” that precipitates catastrophe?
We'll explore these questions through arguably the most controversial of the children of the Enlightenment, the Marquis De Sade. In particular, we'll be focusing on 20th century receptions of Sade's work by European writers and philosophers. Not only will this approach offer us possible answers to the questions above, it will allow us to survey major currents of Continental philosophy in the 20th century. See below for an overview of the writers and topics we'll be discussing.
⸻
**READINGS OVERVIEW**
**Barthes** extends the structuralist approach developed by Levi-Strauss and Lacan (whom we read previously) to literary theory. In his reading, Sade emerges as a *logothete*, a founder of a language and a paradigmatic writer of ""texts of bliss"", whose jouissance derives from the very wounding they precipitate.
**Foucault** redirects the analytical focus away from Barthean semiotics of jouissance and towards what we might call the Sadean episteme. The criterion of the Sadean world emerges as a certain kind of *truth*: anti-metaphysical, anti-theological, anti-moral. In bracketing away reality, this truth elevates desire to a sovereign status within human experience.
**De Beauvoir** argues that we must not burn Sade, though we must approach him with caution. Sade exemplifies the existentialist credos of choosing one's freedom and confronting existence without illusions. He is also one of the first to confront the problem of the Other head-on. Yet his glaring fault is to have succumbed to radical solipsism – a brutal compulsion to efface the Other rather than engage existentially with the singularity of Difference.
**Klossowski** and **Bataille** offer a quasi-theological interpretation of Sade, linking his texts to the subterranean persistence of the sacred in modernity. Bataille develops his central notion of *heterology* with reference to Sade's work.
For **Blanchot**, the Sadean paradox consists in *apathy*: the most radical and extreme actions are not done out of passion but out of detached rational reflection. Such a state of indifference amounts to an absolute sovereignty, the complete separation of the ego from its relations to the world.
**Adorno** and **Horkheimer** find in Sade a prime illustration of their thesis that the calculative rationality of the Enlightenment is cold, objectifying and founded on domination. They regard him as a diabolical truth-teller whose works pre-figure the horrors of totalitarian bureaucracy.
⸻ ♦ ♦ ♦ ⸻
**ABOUT THIS READING GROUP**
This is a comprehensive reading group focusing on 20th century French philosophy. Our current project was stimulated by our recent reading of George Bataille's *Erotism: Death and Sensuality*. Other writers we have discussed include Sartre, de Beauvoir, Foucault, Barthes, Derrida, Deleuze, Kristeva, etc.

**GROUP RULES**

• Please spend 1-2 hours per week reading and preparing for the discussion. Speaking priority will be given to those who have done the readings.
• Keep your comments concise and relevant to the text.
• Please limit each comment to a maximum of 2-3 minutes. You're welcome to speak as many times as you wish.
• Virtual meeting courtesy applies: let's not interrupt each other, keep mics muted when not speaking, and maintain a respectable dress code.
• We'll focus the discussion with key passages and discussion questions. Be sure to bring your favorite passages, questions, comments, criticisms, etc. to the meeting.

