53b031eb-0ce8-4087-86b5-c587d4ab7e40,,HACKATHON - Agent Eval Harness,Hosted by DL Lim,"Hi new friends and old! The Friendly Data Meetup Hackathon is back by popular demand! 🎉

After the brilliant turnout (and the even more brilliant demos) at our first hackathon in January, we simply had to do it again.

👩💻 This evening is all about learning, building, and having fun with friendly faces. No pressure, no pitch decks, just inquisitive minds making cool things with data. With perhaps a slight competitive edge for those seeking glory 😉

📍 We're very grateful to **Quantspark** for kindly hosting us at their venue for the evening!

**💡 The challenge: Judge the Agent ⚖️**

*Dawdle Deliveries'* shiny new AI customer support bot goes live next week. Is it actually ready? Your job is to find out, with evidence instead of vibes!

AI agents are moving into production, and everyone is asking the same question: how do you know it works? Together, we'll find out by building an evaluation harness from scratch:

• 🧪 **Write the tests:** a set of realistic (and sneaky) customer questions
• ✅ **Build the checks:** automatic pass/fail checks, plus an AI judge that marks every answer
• 🔧 **Fix it and prove it:** improve the bot, then show a before/after scorecard proving your fix worked (without breaking anything else!)

The challenge is split into levels, so every team can work at its own pace, and there are bonus rounds (tracing, red-teaming, model shoot-outs) for the speedy ones. We'll finish with quick 3-minute demos: your scorecard, your most interesting failure, and the big question: *do you trust your judge?* 🤔

You’re welcome to choose your own team of 2-4, or teams will be formed on the evening, so don’t worry if you’re coming solo.

**✅ Pre-flight checklist**

This hackathon is very hands-on, so it's best suited to people who already write code and/or have some working knowledge with data and AI. You'll get the most out of the evening if you:

• Have decent working experience writing code
• Are very comfortable with git, Docker and Python environments
• Are happy to dig in and figure things out with your team (organisers will be around to help, but there won't be step-by-step hand-holding)

**🎒 Please bring**

• A laptop (charged!) and charger; preinstalled with: • Docker (https://docs.docker.com/get-docker/) • uv (https://docs.astral.sh/uv/getting-started/installation/) • An LLM of your choice to work with, either: • your own API key for a hosted provider of your choice, or • free local models running via Ollama in Docker (no key needed, but allow around 10 GB of free disk space)
• Curiosity and a willingness to collaborate

🌄 **On that evening**

When you arrive at the venue, show your Luma QR code, and say you're going to Quantspark for the Hackathon.

17:30 - Doors open

18:00 - Hackathon begins

21:00 - Hackathon ends, and building management cut off time

21:01 - Nearby pub!

**📶 Wi-Fi** will be provided at the venue.

We'll share the starter kit with registered participants before the event, so please download everything (especially the local models) in advance. They're a few GB, and the Wi-Fi is no match for a whole room pulling them at once! 😅

**📒IMPORTANT NOTE**

To take part in the hackathon, **please sign up on Luma here**. You will be required to show your QR code at the reception. **RSVP on Meetup does not count as registration for this event**, so sign up on Luma now to secure your place!

Spaces are limited, so please let us know if you can't make it, so that we may free your seat up for someone else :)

Many thanks for your enthusiasm and participation! We can't wait to build with you 😄

If you like this event, check out our first ever fireside chat happening in November too:",https://images.lumacdn.com/uploads/dc/f0a16d21-d977-4b25-9d28-4a7dbec915bf.png,2026-11-04 17:30:00+00,2026-11-04 21:00:00+00,Europe/London,in_person,230 Blackfriars Rd,"230 Blackfriars Rd, London",London,d529a6f8-b2d5-4689-bad4-376fffe4d42a,"{technology,socialising,marketing,careers,ai}",,external,https://luma.com/h8zer2ek,Register on Luma,f,,t,,0.00,0.00,GBP,published,,f,f,2026-10-22 17:08:42.693737+00,source:5f864383-431a-4751-b4a4-f07868d73687,https://luma.com/h8zer2ek,evt-6tmN87665vaD1pb@events.lu.ma,,2026-10-28 03:23:37.327+00,,2026-10-22 17:08:42.693737+00,2026-10-28 03:23:43.085408+00,51.5059586,-0.104151,,,f,GB
5cd83be1-7533-48b3-bcde-258373971eca,,Is AI making us less creative?: An Oxford-style debate,"Hosted by Eleanor, Shreman Shrestha and 1 other","**This house believes AI is making us less creative.**

The machines may have made us faster. Whether they can create something truly novel is another question, one we are settling the old-fashioned way, with an Oxford-style debate.

We'll take an audience vote on the motion when you arrive and again after closing arguments. The winner is the one that moves the most votes. So come with an opinion and be prepared to change your mind.

Expect sharp arguments and, as always, plenty of time to meet the other founders, writers, and founder-writers in the room afterwards.

**Final speaker list to be announced.**

**Organizers**

• **Alys Key:** is editor of The Londoner. She was previously features editor at Digital Frontier and before that was deputy money and business editor at The i Paper. She also writes about technology and politics on her Substack UK 2.0 (https://uk20.substack.com/?utm_source=luma).
• **Eleanor Warnock**: a writer and communications expert who writes a newsletter on AI and writing (https://www.eleanot.es/?utm_source=luma). She has written for The Wall Street Journal and Bloomberg, and was deputy editor at Sifted and managing editor at the Reid Hoffman-backed AI publication Every.

**Speakers**

• **Archie Hall** is *The Economist*'s acting economics editor*.* Alongside running the paper's global economics coverage, he also writes regularly on the political, economic and social implications of AI. Before *The Economist*, he was a macro investor at Bridgewater Associates. He blogs, intermittently, at https://notes.archie-hall.com/.
• **Bruno Marnette** is a repeat entrepreneur with an Oxford PhD who was part of the technical leadership at Meta and Palantir. He is an expert in AI products augmenting creativity and critical thinking, and runs the Taste Bench Project, an independent research initiative focused on understanding, measuring, and improving the taste-adjacent capabilities of AI.
• **Marzia Niccolai** spent 19 years at Google building products from App Engine to Arts & Culture, where she helped photograph the world's artworks at ultra-high resolution and later shipped its first generative AI experiences. She co-founded an AI finance app Francis and is currently working on continuous learning AI at Fractal Brain.
• **Paloma Ruiz** is a sociologist and communicator who works at the intersection of gender equity, culture, and change. Her path has taken her through creative agencies, marketing teams, human rights collectives, and corporate consultancy, and she brings all of it into the workshops, sessions, and talks she leads, spaces designed to open new perspectives. As a researcher, she explores feminist collective action and how music and sound become tools of resistance.

**About The Founder-writer Collective**

Writers are now founders. From Emily Sundberg's ""studio mindset"" (https://www.semafor.com/article/11/17/2024/feed-mes-emily-sundberg-and-her-studio-mindset?utm_source=luma) to Ashlee Vance's bet on independent storytelling (https://www.corememory.com/p/so-begins-core-memory-a-new-sci-tech?utm_source=luma), professional writers own and operate their own brands.

At the same time, company founders increasingly see the value in being writing-first practitioners (https://every.to/p/the-heyday-of-the-writing-first-practitioner?utm_source=luma). By thinking and writing in public, they create new opportunities and cultivate a point of view. In the tradition of Warren Buffett and Paul Graham, by way of Julie Zhuo, founders are now writers.

Since our founding in June, 2026, the Founder-Writer Collective has brought together over 150 curious professionals in person to discuss the future of writing, editing, and content creation as a professional tool, and has built a digital community of over 600. Join our mailing list (https://luma.com/founder-writers) to find out about future events.

This event is generously hosted by Granola (https://www.granola.ai/).",https://res.cloudinary.com/deor01if5/image/upload/v1792688968/orbit/event-covers/sources/tqjedmlc5lswbzdmtlnd.jpg,2026-10-29 18:30:00+00,2026-10-29 20:30:00+00,Europe/London,in_person,Granola,"346 Old St, London",London,7aa69eeb-df8c-40ae-b929-a4324f936266,"{technology,socialising,marketing,careers,creative,ai,books}",,external,https://luma.com/rek0kyyz,Register on Luma,f,,t,,0.00,0.00,GBP,published,,f,f,2026-10-22 17:08:42.49625+00,source:5f864383-431a-4751-b4a4-f07868d73687,https://luma.com/rek0kyyz,evt-TvVukFlc8ZXL9Zk@events.lu.ma,,2026-10-28 03:23:37.327+00,,2026-10-22 17:08:42.49625+00,2026-10-28 03:23:43.085408+00,51.52673,-0.0809555,,,f,GB
