9f0821b3-563e-4257-8307-535070ad2f86,,LDNUG November 2026 with Ana Rodrigues & David Whitney,"Our next meetup will be on November 3rd at Dojo's canalside offices in the Brunel Building, right next to Paddington Station. We've got two fantastic talks lined up from two awesome speakers, plus…","Our next meetup will be on November 3rd at Dojo's canalside offices in the Brunel Building, right next to Paddington Station. We've got two fantastic talks lined up from two awesome speakers, plus food, drinks, plenty of time to chat & network with fellow attendees, and quite probably a drink or two at the pub afterwards.

**Ana Rodrigues: I Spared No Expense**

A talk about the projects I built for my own tiny corner of the web that I did not need, that nobody asked for, and that I built anyway despite my bank account telling me not to. Some of them still work. All of them have a story. No expense was spared.
For anyone who loves personal websites, silly side projects, and tech built for the joy of it rather than the job of it. There's code on screen throughout, but the real point is a nudge to remember that programming can be a hobby, not just a career.

**Ana** works as a front-end developer for the agency Hactar. She started coding as a teenager building fan sites, and has been working as a front-end developer for the last 13 years. Nowadays, Ana spends most of her free time experimenting on her personal blog and is particularly interested in ethics, IndieWeb, sustainability, plants, cooking, privacy and all things CSS.

**David Whitney: Why not both? Bringing Modern Web Development to .NET**

This talk is about the process of building three ""what if"" experiments that turned into a real projects to bring popular libraries, languages and tools to the .NET ecosystem.

• JsxCore - Native .NET bindings for ASP.NET MVC and React and JSX.
• AstroSharp - C# support for the popular Astro Static site builder
• TsxCore - a TypeScript compiler that targets MSIL and
the CLR that compiles Typescript programs to native .NET

We'll cover how these seemingly impossible projects work, how they were designed, and why picking the best tool for the job sometimes takes you to really weird places.

A fun deep dive where you'll learn how to bridge seemingly incompatible platforms, build framework-like tools, and hopefully inspire you to make things you like play together. A talk about having your cake, and eating it.

**David** is the Director of Architecture for NewDay, and the founder of Electric Head Software. Focusing on iterative software delivery, developer mentoring and cultural change - mostly working with London-based organisations.

He speaks about software design, culture, and ethics in technology - rounded out by an assortment of talks about software that probably doesn't need to exist but makes the world a little more fun. David has previously served as the chief coding technical architect for JustGiving and helped market-leading organisations including JUST-EAT, Trainline and Vodafone improve their technical capabilities.

David is a Microsoft MVP, has been part of the OpenUK Honours list for open-source advocacy, and is a twice bestselling author of children's books about programming.",https://secure.meetupstatic.com/photos/event/e/4/7/b/highres_536398491.jpeg,2026-11-03 18:30:00+00,,Europe/London,in_person,Dojo,"The Brunel Building, 2 Canalside Walk, London W2 1DG, UK",London,c78f921f-ce89-4a92-b98f-83bf7cc57102,"{technology,careers,food}",,external,https://www.meetup.com/london-net-user-group/events/316797594/,Register on Meetup,f,,t,,,,GBP,published,,f,f,2026-10-22 19:56:05.832408+00,source:a123de80-56fb-42df-942f-e46132c725f9,https://www.meetup.com/london-net-user-group/events/316797594/,event_316797594@meetup.com,,2026-10-28 03:22:57.772+00,,2026-10-22 19:56:05.832408+00,2026-10-28 03:23:34.493162+00,,,,,f,GB
