9690fcd7-cb92-4d43-9a54-9de3ad8ff992,,Bradbury's Fahreheit 451 & Truffaut's film adaptation,"Ray Bradbury's Fahrenheit 451 became his most famous novel and one of the defining works of twentieth-century dystopian literature. Published in 1953, the novel emerged during the tense early Cold…","Ray Bradbury's *Fahrenheit 451* became his most famous novel and one of the defining works of twentieth-century dystopian literature. Published in 1953, the novel emerged during the tense early Cold War, when fear of communism, political conformity and ideolical suspicion shaped American life. Against this background, Bradbury imagined a society where books are forbidden and firemen burn them rather than extiguishing fires. The novel became a powerful exploration of censorship, conformity and the suppression of independent thought.

The title refers to the temperature at which according to Bradbury's original claim, book paper catches fire, though its scientific accuracy has been questioned. More important is fire's symbolic ambiguity: it represents destruction, but also warmth, illumination and renewal. The meaning becomes central to Montag's journey as he questions the world he has accepted and discovers that knowledge can survive the destruction of physical books. Thus, *Fahrenheit 451* is not simply a political novel about government censorship. Books disappear not only because the state destroys them, but because society loses its desire to read. The novel, therefore, asks not only what happens when goverments ban books, but what happens when people themselves stop wanting complexity, ambiguity and uncomforable knowledge.

Born in Waukegan, Illinois, in 1920, Bradbury grew up during the Great Depression and became a prolific writer of science fiction, fantasy and speculative fiction. Unable to attend university, he educated himself through extensive reading in libraries where he also developped his writing. Although he became one of the greatest voices of twentieth-century science fiction, Bradbury disliked being described simply as a science-fiction writer. He saw himself as a writer of ideas, imagination and the human experience- and *Fahrenheit 451* is a perfect example of that broader vision. Its exactly this 'broader vision' of his that connects *Fahrenheit 451* to Francois Truffaut's 1966 film adaptation (his first and only English film).

Truffaut, a central figure of the French New Wave, approaches the novel not simply as a conventional science-fiction story but as an artistic and philosophical work, using the expressive possibilities of cinema to create an atmosphere of unease, isolation and intellectual repression.
Using stark monochrome imagery, atmospheric cinematography, and distinctly European cinematic sensibility, Truffaut's film offers an intriguing interpretation of Bradbury's novel.

Truffaut was a former film critic who had writen extensively about directors and cinema before becoming a filmmaker. His *Fahrenheit 451* therfore becomes, in part, a film about the value of art itself. If Bradbury asks what happens to society that loses its relationship with books; Truffaut asks related question through cinema: what happens to human beings when art, imagination and critical thought are replaced by passive entertainment? His bold visual choices, unusual rhythms, and sensitive attention to character demonstrate the creativity of a director who helped redefine modern filmmaking.

Together, Bradbury's literary imagination and Truffaut's cinematic vision make *Fahrenheit 451* a vital meeting point between literature and film, inviting us to consider not only what happens when books disappear, but also why stories, ideas, and the freedom to question remain essential to a living culture.
Both the novel and the film ask whether society can remain free if its citizens become unwilling or unable to engage with ideas that challenge them.

Join our Modern Books and Cinema Club to discover the brilliance of Bradbury and Truffaut and discuss why their works continue to speak so powerfully to audiences today.",https://secure.meetupstatic.com/photos/event/2/e/9/d/highres_536351933.jpeg,2026-11-02 18:00:00+00,,Europe/Athens,online,Online event,,,c17c4a77-8c19-42da-a932-7fd57ad27fc7,"{social,books,creative,socialising}",,external,https://www.meetup.com/modern-books-and-cinema-club/events/316745654/,Register on Meetup,f,,t,,,,GBP,published,,f,f,2026-10-22 19:46:37.169472+00,source:c243439e-e165-41fe-9fdf-b8e7ca9d670a,https://www.meetup.com/modern-books-and-cinema-club/events/316745654/,event_316745654@meetup.com,,2026-10-28 03:19:10.809+00,,2026-10-22 19:46:37.169472+00,2026-10-28 03:19:47.776315+00,,,,,f,
b115e563-1de7-4fe3-8c50-76a869dc70a0,,The Book Club in London,"A relaxed London book club exploring the most influential book in history. Whether you think it’s rubbish, holy, or you’re simply not sure what to make of it, you’re very welcome.","A relaxed London book club exploring the most influential book in history. Whether you think it’s rubbish, holy, or you’re simply not sure what to make of it, you’re very welcome.

We meet for two hours at a time. Our reading list includes books from the Bible — as well as some of the books that didn’t make it into most Bibles. The Bible has shaped culture, law, art and ethics for centuries, yet many people are personally unfamiliar with it.

Each evening is structured but informal. The group chooses the book or books to read, and discussion takes place in small groups. No lectures. No pressure. No need to prepare anything in advance. The aim is thoughtful, respectful conversation.

Plenty of people feel they know quite a bit about the Bible, yet struggle to read it. This is a space to explore it thoughtfully and honestly — in good company.
This club is for:
• Curious thinkers
• Skeptics
• Christians with questions
• Anyone who wants to make up their own mind

Our only rules?
Be curious.
Be honest.
Be kind.
And enjoy yourself.

It’s also a chance to find a bit of community along the way.

Photos may be taken during the evening to share on the Meetup page or social media to help others see what the group is like. If you prefer not to appear in photos, just let the organiser know at any time.

We meet in a private room on the second floor of the Duke of Sussex pub. When you arrive, get yourself a drink, ask for The Book Club, and come on up.",https://res.cloudinary.com/deor01if5/image/upload/v1792898422/orbit/event-covers/sources/atz1se8gvp8p1essc1py.jpg,2026-10-26 18:30:00+00,,Europe/London,in_person,"Baylis Road, London, SE1 7AY",Baylis Road,London,bbf01bc7-85fe-4d0e-8f0c-8fd50e9ffd7e,"{social,books,socialising,arts}",,external,https://www.meetup.com/the-book-club-in-london/events/316496223/,Register on Meetup,f,,t,,,,GBP,published,,f,f,2026-10-22 19:46:37.169472+00,source:c243439e-e165-41fe-9fdf-b8e7ca9d670a,https://www.meetup.com/the-book-club-in-london/events/316496223/,event_316496223@meetup.com,,2026-10-26 03:19:10.789+00,,2026-10-22 19:46:37.169472+00,2026-10-26 03:19:42.346903+00,,,source:c243439e-e165-41fe-9fdf-b8e7ca9d670a:the book club in london:baylis road london se1 7ay,,f,GB
