006efbd5-3908-4a18-85a7-c0d5d9cfc3cf,,City Photowalk,"We had a great turnout last time and would love to keep the momentum. Given that the weather is getting a bit iffy, I’m going to come up with a Plan A for the actual walk and Plan B if it literally…","We had a great turnout last time and would love to keep the momentum. Given that the weather is getting a bit iffy, I’m going to come up with a Plan A for the actual walk and Plan B if it literally rains on our photography parade.
See you guys soon!

**Photowalk Code of Conduct**

Our club is a welcoming, inclusive and judgment-free space for photographers of every background and experience level.

1. Everyone is equal. Treat every member with the same respect, regardless of age, race, ethnicity, nationality, religion, gender, sexuality, disability, appearance, experience or equipment.
2. Be kind. Bullying, harassment, discrimination, intimidation, exclusion or deliberately making someone uncomfortable will not be tolerated.
3. No shaming. Never shame anyone for their photographs, ability, camera, phone, editing style, knowledge or level of experience.
4. Do not make negative comments about ability. Everyone is here to enjoy photography and develop at their own pace. Constructive feedback should only be given when someone asks for it, and it must be respectful and helpful.
5. Respect different styles. Photography is subjective. You do not have to like someone’s work, but you must respect their creative choices.
6. Help, don’t compete. Celebrate other people’s progress, share knowledge generously and make newer members feel welcome. This club is a community, not a competition.
7. Respect personal boundaries. Ask before photographing another member or sharing an identifiable photograph of them. If someone asks not to be photographed, respect that immediately.
8. Include everyone. Do not form cliques, deliberately leave people behind during walks or make anyone feel that they do not belong.
9. Take responsibility for your impact. Jokes and comments should not come at somebody else’s expense. If someone tells you that your behaviour made them uncomfortable, listen and respond respectfully.
10. Speak to an organiser if something happens. Concerns will be taken seriously and handled as fairly and discreetly as possible. Serious or repeated breaches may result in removal from the club.

The basic rule: be kind, be inclusive and never make another person feel small.",https://secure.meetupstatic.com/photos/event/a/5/c/highres_535502652.jpeg,2027-03-06 14:00:00+00,2027-03-06 16:00:00+00,Europe/London,in_person,Liverpool Street,Liverpool Street,London,bbf01bc7-85fe-4d0e-8f0c-8fd50e9ffd7e,"{social,creative,socialising}",,external,https://www.meetup.com/photowalk-club/events/wmhcbvykcfbjb/,Register on Meetup,f,,t,,,,GBP,published,,f,f,2026-10-22 19:46:36.725985+00,source:c243439e-e165-41fe-9fdf-b8e7ca9d670a,https://www.meetup.com/photowalk-club/events/wmhcbvykcfbjb/,event_wmhcbvykcfbjb@meetup.com,,2026-10-28 03:19:10.809+00,,2026-10-22 19:46:36.725985+00,2026-10-28 03:19:47.193092+00,,,meetup:photowalk-club:city photowalk,Every 1st Saturday of the month,f,GB
6f872be2-0aae-4a55-b64c-ded61312eb28,,Build a Reasoning Model (From Scratch),We are working through Build a Reasoning Model (From Scratch) by Sebastian Raschka.,"We are working through *Build a Reasoning Model (From Scratch)* by Sebastian Raschka.
During each meetup, we will discuss the key concepts from the chapter or pages under review. In this session we'll discuss the **Chapter 6. Training Reasoning Models with Reinforcement Learning.**

Raschka provides a hands-on guide to understanding and building modern reasoning models. Starting with a conventional pretrained LLM, the book explores evaluation, inference-time reasoning techniques, reinforcement learning with verifiable rewards, and knowledge distillation.

Please note that the session is not recorded, and participants are responsible for obtaining their own copy of the book.

**Buy the book** (affiliate links)**:**
Amazon UK (https://amzn.to/4qecrce)
Amazon France (https://link.amazon/B00LK2CWI)

**Book overview:**
In *Build a Reasoning Model (From Scratch)*, bestselling author Sebastian Raschka explains how modern reasoning-oriented language models work by implementing their core techniques step by step.
You will begin with a conventional pretrained LLM and learn how to generate and evaluate its responses. You will then explore inference-time techniques such as chain-of-thought prompting, sampling, self-consistency, response scoring, Best-of-N, and self-refinement. Later chapters introduce training-based approaches, including reinforcement learning with verifiable rewards, GRPO, format rewards, and distillation from stronger reasoning models into smaller ones.
*Build a Reasoning Model (From Scratch)* teaches you how to:

• Implement core LLM reasoning techniques from scratch
• Generate, score, and evaluate model responses
• Build verifier-based evaluation systems
• Improve reasoning using self-consistency, Best-of-N, and self-refinement
• Apply reinforcement learning with verifiable rewards
• Understand and implement GRPO-based training
• Distill the reasoning capabilities of stronger models into smaller ones
• Evaluate the accuracy, cost, and latency trade-offs of different reasoning techniques",https://secure.meetupstatic.com/photos/event/8/8/6/b/highres_535654923.jpeg,2026-11-08 15:30:00+00,,Europe/London,online,Online event,,,4cece675-2670-4581-90e6-2b792576b047,{technology},,external,https://www.meetup.com/llm-reading-club/events/317142795/,Register on Meetup,f,,t,,,,GBP,published,,f,f,2026-10-28 03:23:34.220625+00,source:a123de80-56fb-42df-942f-e46132c725f9,https://www.meetup.com/llm-reading-club/events/317142795/,event_317142795@meetup.com,,2026-10-28 03:22:57.772+00,,2026-10-28 03:23:34.220625+00,2026-10-28 03:23:36.553837+00,,,,,f,
